<TopicPage slug="subjects/radiography/foundational/x-ray-physics-and-safety">

  <h2>Where you are, and what you will learn</h2>
  <p>
    This is the opening lesson of Radiography, and it assumes no physics beyond everyday
    familiarity with electricity and multiplication — everything is built here. The physics is
    unusually learnable because it is honest: the same handful of principles explains the
    machine's dials, the image's greys, the department's lead aprons and the radiographer's own
    standing position.
  </p>
  <p>By the end you will be able to:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>describe how an X-ray tube makes radiation from fast, suddenly stopped electrons;</li>
    <li>explain how a shadow image forms from differential absorption, and what the two dials — kVp and mAs — do to it;</li>
    <li>distinguish deterministic from stochastic radiation effects, and read doses in gray and sievert;</li>
    <li>apply the time–distance–shielding triad and state what ALARA demands in daily practice;</li>
    <li>justify each exposure: benefit weighed against the dose it adds.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    One habit: every time the word "dose" appears, we ask <em>dose to whom</em> — the patient
    or the practitioner — because the physics answers differently for each.
  </p>

  <h2>A shadow, not a photograph</h2>
  <p>
    When X-rays were discovered in 1895, the first medical images spread around the world
    within months — a hand wearing a ring, its bones dark against pale flesh. The picture is
    best understood as a shadow, and that is the single most useful sentence in this lesson.
    Light from the sun casts a shadow because your body blocks light. An X-ray image casts a
    shadow because different tissues block X-rays <em>differently</em>: bone blocks a lot,
    soft tissue some, air almost nothing. What reaches the detector on the far side of the
    body is a pattern of "more blocked" and "less blocked" — and that pattern is the picture.
  </p>
  <p>
    Radiography is the discipline of making such shadows — safely. Wrapped around the physics
    is an ethic unique to the job: unlike light or sound, X-rays carry a small but real
    capacity for harm, so every exposure must be <em>justified</em> — it must change or confirm
    the diagnosis enough to be worth the dose it adds. That ethic has a name, <strong>ALARA</strong>
    (As Low As Reasonably Achievable), and it is not a poster on the wall; it is the operating
    procedure of the whole profession, rebuilt into every exposure factor, every position and
    every room layout.
  </p>

  <h2>X-ray production: electrons, decelerated</h2>
  <p>
    An X-ray tube is a vacuum tube with two ends. At the <strong>cathode</strong>, a filament
    is heated until electrons boil off it (thermionic emission) and gather in a small cloud.
    Across a gap of a few centimetres, a potential difference of tens of thousands of volts —
    the <strong>kilovoltage peak, kVp</strong> — accelerates them toward the
    <strong>anode</strong>, a spinning disc of tungsten alloy. When the electrons slam into the
    target at around half the speed of light, their sudden deceleration releases energy as
    X-ray photons — and it happens two ways.
  </p>
  <Widget no="3" caption="rebuild the production sequence" widget="order" config={radTubeOrder} />
  <p>
    Most photons are <strong>bremsstrahlung</strong> ("braking radiation"): an electron skims
    past a tungsten nucleus, is deflected, and radiates away whatever energy it lost — which
    can be anything from almost nothing up to the full beam voltage. The result is a continuous
    spectrum from zero up to the kVp, the honest signature of random braking. A minority of
    photons are <strong>characteristic radiation</strong>: occasionally an incoming electron
    knocks a tungsten inner-shell electron out entirely, and an outer-shell electron falls into
    the vacancy, emitting a photon of a precise energy. Characteristic lines depend on the
    target material, which is why tungsten (high atomic number, high melting point) is the
    target of choice — and why mammography machines use molybdenum targets instead, shifting
    the spectrum to softer energies that contrast soft tissue better.
  </p>
  <Widget no="4" caption="pair each spectral term with its mechanism" widget="match" config={radSpectrumMatch} />
  <p>
    The beam that leaves the port is already a designed product: an aluminium filter absorbs
    the useless low-energy photons that would dose the patient's skin without reaching the
    detector, and collimator blades trim the field to the anatomy needed. Two dials then shape
    every exposure: <strong>kVp</strong> sets the beam's <em>quality</em> — its penetrating
    power, and with it the image contrast and scatter behaviour; <strong>mAs</strong> (tube
    current multiplied by exposure time) sets the beam's <em>quantity</em> — how many photons,
    and with it, linearly, how much dose. Everything else in image production is the management
    of these two levers.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="how X-rays are made, absorbed and justified" widget="equationStep" config={safetyCfg} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> the five panels are this whole lesson in order —
    production, absorption, cumulative dose, the protection triad, and ALARA on top. Notice
    especially panel 3: the justification step sits <em>before</em> any technique choice,
    because no combination of dials can make an unnecessary exposure worthwhile. The rest of
    this page simply unpacks each panel.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> an X-ray tube converts electrical energy into a filtered, focused
    beam of photons whose penetration (kVp) and quantity (mAs) are set deliberately. The beam
    now meets the patient — and the image is made in the meeting.
  </p>

  <h2>Exposure factors and the image: absorption is the picture</h2>
  <p>
    As the beam crosses the body, photons are removed by interactions that depend on the
    tissue's density and effective atomic number: bone (calcium, high atomic number) absorbs
    far more than muscle and fat, which absorb more than air. Two mechanisms do the work. The
    <strong>photoelectric effect</strong> — a photon fully absorbed, an electron ejected —
    scales steeply with atomic number (approximately as the cube), and it produces the crisp
    bone-versus-soft-tissue contrast that carries most diagnoses. <strong>Compton
    scattering</strong> — a photon deflected, sharing its energy — depends mostly on electron
    density, dominates at higher energies, and produces <em>scatter</em>: photons flying in all
    directions that fog the image from every angle.
  </p>
  <Widget no="5" caption="file each fact under its interaction" widget="sort-buckets" config={radInteractSort} />
  <p>
    This is the physics behind the exposure-factor logic radiographers use daily. Raising kVp
    increases penetration and Compton scatter, reducing subject contrast and skin dose; lowering
    it boosts photoelectric contrast until penetration fails. The trade has rules of thumb: the
    15 per cent rule (raising kVp by 15 per cent approximately doubles the exposure the detector
    receives, so kVp can be traded against mAs) and the inverse square law linking intensity to
    source-to-image distance — a standard chest radiograph is taken at 180 cm rather than
    100 cm, which flatters the heart's size and minimises geometric blur. Scatter is managed
    with a <strong>grid</strong> of thin lead strips between patient and detector that absorbs
    the obliquely travelling photons (at the cost of more mAs), and with tight collimation that
    reduces scatter at its source.
  </p>
  <p>
    Digital detectors changed the economics of the greys: where film had a narrow tolerance and
    under- or over-exposed films had to be repeated, modern detectors respond over a huge range
    and the computer rescales any exposure into a viewable image. The hidden price is
    <strong>dose creep</strong> — because a digitally over-exposed image still looks fine, the
    feedback that once punished excess dose has vanished, and departments must police exposure
    indices deliberately. Automatic exposure control (radiation-measuring chambers behind the
    patient that cut the exposure at a target dose) exists to keep that discipline objective.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> that an X-ray machine is a kind of camera — that it
  "photographs" the inside by something bouncing back to the machine. Nothing returns: the
  detector sits <em>behind</em> the patient, and the image is made by what gets
  <em>through</em>. This is why the discipline is called projection imaging, why the patient is
  positioned between tube and detector, and why thickness, positioning and penetration matter
  so much — the shadow can only be as good as the beam that casts it.</p>
  <Widget no="6" caption="find the faulty line — an imaging briefing" widget="spot-error" config={radCameraSpot} />

  <h2>Dose: how harm is counted</h2>
  <p>
    Radiation biology separates two kinds of harm, and the separation organises all of safety.
    <strong>Deterministic effects</strong> have thresholds — skin reddening around 2 Gy
    (cataracts far higher) — and diagnostic radiography sits nowhere near them; they belong to
    fluoroscopy accidents and radiotherapy errors. <strong>Stochastic effects</strong> — cancer
    induction chiefly — have no observed threshold and are modelled as probability rising with
    dose: no single exposure causes a specific harm, but each adds a small increment of
    lifetime <em>risk</em>. Stochastic risk is the reason for the field's conservatism.
  </p>
  <p>
    The units track the distinction. The <strong>gray</strong> (Gy) measures absorbed energy;
    the <strong>sievert</strong> (Sv) weights it by biological effectiveness; <em>effective
    dose</em> in mSv converts a given exposure into a comparable whole-body risk figure. The
    scale gives radiography's honest context: a standard chest radiograph delivers about
    0.02 mSv — days of natural background; a CT abdomen, roughly 8–10 mSv; average natural
    background itself, around 2.4 mSv per year. The comparisons do not make dose trivial —
    they make justification and optimisation meaningful.
  </p>
  <Widget no="7" caption="call the comparison count" widget="estimate" config={radCtEst} />
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> that radiation "stays in your body" after an X-ray — that
  the patient becomes radioactive, or that doses accumulate physically like a poison. The
  photons pass through or are absorbed and are gone in fractions of a second; nothing
  radioactive is implanted. What is genuinely cumulative is the <em>risk statistics</em>: each
  exposure adds a small increment of lifetime stochastic risk, which is why dose history is
  recorded — but "how long until it wears off?" has no physical meaning.</p>
  <Widget no="8" caption="fill the blanks — what actually accumulates" widget="cloze" config={radCumulCloze} />

  <h2>ALARA in practice: time, distance, shielding</h2>
  <p>
    ALARA's daily tools are the classic triad: <strong>time, distance, shielding</strong> —
    plus the engineering controls that act before anyone stands anywhere. Collimation and
    filtration cut the dose at its source; grids and tight beam restriction cut scatter; and
    the <strong>inverse square law</strong> governs the practitioner's own position: intensity
    falls with the <em>square</em> of distance, so doubling your distance from the source cuts
    your dose to a quarter. The calculator below makes that concrete.
  </p>

  <Widget no="2" caption="inverse square: intensity vs distance" widget="sliderLab" config={squareCfg} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> leave intensity at 10 mGy and move distance 2 from 100 cm
    to 140 cm — the readout falls to about half (10 ÷ 1.96 ≈ 5.1 mGy, since 1.4 squared is
    1.96). Push to 180 cm and it falls to about a third of the starting value, not a half or a
    two-thirds:
    distance is the cheapest protection in radiology because it compounds <em>quadratically</em>.
    During fluoroscopy, where the beam runs for minutes, these habits — stepping back, standing
    behind the barrier, wearing the apron (0.25–0.5 mm lead equivalent), monitoring personal
    dosimeters — are the difference between a career dose and a regulatory problem.
  </p>
  <p>
    For patients, the same logic drives the protocols: paediatric techniques (children are more
    radiosensitive and have more decades for stochastic effects to appear), pregnancy screening
    and the ten-day rule for abdominopelvic imaging, and the reflex of asking, before every
    exposure: is this projection, this collimation, this technique the minimum that still
    answers the clinical question? The profession exists where physics meets people — anxious
    patients who cannot hold still, injuries needing special projections, children — and the
    judgement of technique against body habitus is human. Image quality, patient care and
    radiation safety are one job, which is why the registry certifies a person, not a machine
    operator.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> that doubling your distance from an X-ray source halves
  your dose. It <em>reduces</em> it, and that feels right — most quantities in life do scale
  linearly. But radiation spreads over a sphere whose area grows with the square of the
  radius, so double the distance means one <em>quarter</em> of the intensity, and ten times
  the distance means one hundredth. The whole triad of protection works this way: time cuts
  dose linearly, distance cuts it quadratically, shielding cuts it by orders of magnitude.</p>

  <h2>Check yourself</h2>
  <p>Try these before moving on. Feedback follows each answer.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Why does bone appear pale on a radiograph while air appears dark?</li>
    <li>kVp and mAs: which sets penetration, which sets quantity, and what happens to contrast when kVp rises?</li>
    <li>A chest X-ray delivers about 0.02 mSv. Does the patient need to declare it at an airport security check? Why not?</li>
    <li>You are assisting in fluoroscopy. List three things you can do, in order of effectiveness per effort, to cut your own dose.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    <strong>Feedback:</strong> (1) Bone's calcium and effective atomic number give strong
    photoelectric absorption, so few photons reach the detector behind it — pale shadow. Air
    absorbs almost nothing, so the detector behind it receives nearly the full beam — dark.
    (2) kVp sets quality (penetration and, with it, contrast and scatter); mAs sets quantity
    (photons and dose). Raising kVp increases penetration and Compton scatter, which lowers
    subject contrast. (3) No — the patient is not radioactive: photons are absorbed or pass
    through and are gone at once. There is nothing to emit, which is exactly why the "how long
    does it stay in me?" question has no physical meaning. (4) Step back (inverse square:
    double distance, quarter dose); stand behind the mobile barrier; minimise time in the beam
    and wear your lead apron and dosimeter — the apron is last because it is worn constantly,
    while distance and barrier do the heavy work per second of exposure.
  </p>

  <h2>Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>X-rays are made by boiling electrons off a filament and slamming them into a tungsten target: bremsstrahlung gives a continuous spectrum, characteristic lines give precise energies.</li>
    <li>kVp sets beam quality (penetration, contrast, scatter); mAs sets quantity (photons, dose).</li>
    <li>The image is a shadow made by differential absorption: photoelectric effect (scales with atomic number cubed) gives contrast; Compton scatter gives fog.</li>
    <li>Deterministic effects have thresholds; stochastic effects are probabilistic and cumulative as lifetime risk. Gray measures absorbed energy, sievert weights it biologically.</li>
    <li>Time, distance and shielding: distance compounds quadratically — double the distance, one quarter the dose.</li>
    <li>ALARA — as low as reasonably achievable — governs every exposure: justified, optimised, and never casually repeated.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where this goes next</h2>
  <p>
    The physics here underlies everything that follows. Patient care (next unit) adds consent,
    communication and infection control around every exposure. Image production and quality
    extends the kVp–mAs logic into digital detectors, positioning and artefacts. Radiographic
    procedures apply the whole package anatomy by anatomy. In each, the questions fixed here
    recur unchanged: is this exposure justified, is the technique optimised, is the dose — for
    patient and practitioner alike — as low as reasonably achievable?
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    The exposure arithmetic deserves one fully worked passage, because it is the daily
    reasoning of the profession. Suppose a knee radiograph is correctly exposed at 60 kVp and
    5 mAs, and the next patient is both larger and unable to hold still. The radiographer
    raises kVp by 15 per cent — 60 to 69 kVp — which approximately doubles the receptor
    exposure, so mAs can drop to 2.5 for the same detector signal: higher penetration for the
    thicker part, half the dose quantity, and half the exposure time, which also halves the
    motion-blur risk in the uncooperative patient. Every element of the trade is visible: kVp
    bought penetration and speed, mAs paid the dose bill, and the motion constraint chose where
    the saving landed. Reverse the case — a fine-detail extremity view on a thin, still patient
    — and the trade reverses: lower kVp for maximal subject contrast, more mAs accepted,
    because sharp grey-scale separation matters more than dose economy on a milligray
    examination. Technique charts simply store hundreds of such resolved trades.
  </p>
  <Widget no="9" caption="drag the divider — two resolved trades" widget="compare" config={radTradeCompare} />
  <p>
    Three refinements take you from technique charts to understanding. First,
    <strong>beam vocabulary</strong>: the half-value layer (HVL) — the absorber thickness that
    halves the beam — is the practical measure of penetrating quality, and its arithmetic is
    beautifully simple: stack identical absorbers and each one halves what is left, so four
    halvings leave one sixteenth of the original beam. That is why thin lead aprons, a few
    tenths of a millimetre thick, reduce scattered diagnostic radiation to a small fraction
    while remaining wearable all day. The anode heel effect — intensity varying along the
    anode–cathode axis because the target's own slant absorbs part of the exit beam — is
    exploited daily by placing the cathode end over thicker anatomy. Second, <strong>contrast
    media weaponise the photoelectric effect</strong>: iodine and barium have absorption edges
    tuned almost exactly to the diagnostic spectrum, which is why a few milligrams per
    millilitre of them turn vessels and bowels luminous — a designed application of the
    atomic-number scaling. Third, the <strong>dose–risk debate is live</strong>: the linear
    no-threshold model behind stochastic risk estimates is an extrapolation from
    atomic-bomb-survivor data down to doses radiography actually uses, where epidemiology
    cannot resolve the risk; the profession's answer is not to argue the slope but to make the
    debate moot — justification frameworks, collective dose discipline, and detectors that
    keep dropping the dose per image.
  </p>
  <Widget no="10" caption="true or false — the refinements" widget="judge" config={radRefineJudge} />
  <p>
    History closes the loop. For a period in the mid-twentieth century, shoe shops used
    unshielded fluoroscopes to show customers their toes inside new shoes — machines that ran
    continuously, exposed feet to far more than any medical image, and fascinated children,
    until radiation-protection laws shut them down in the 1950s. The episode is remembered in
    the profession not as an embarrassment but as a founding lesson: the physics of 1895 has
    been optimised for over a century, but it was the ethics — the first radiographers burned
    their hands before anyone understood dose — that turned the physics into a profession.
  </p>

  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> ARRT Radiography Content Specifications; Bushong, <em>Radiologic Science for Technologists</em>; ICRP dose recommendations. Source text: OpenStax (https://openstax.org), CC BY 4.0 — adapted for Interactive Atlas.</p>

